<h1 class="ph">How to tell an API-key failure from a broken MCP server ([[SMX:CD_FMT]] check)</h1>
<p class="answer">As of [[SMX:CD_FMT_FULL]], [[SMX:PASS]] of [[SMX:TOTAL]] sandboxed installs passed ([[SMX:RATE]]% of the [[SMX:TESTED]] judgeable installs).[[SMX:BASE_SENTENCE]] Another [[SMX:NEEDS_KEY]] installs failed solely because our keyless test sandbox has no API keys or logins, while [[SMX:BROKEN]] installs failed two nights running for other reasons.</p>

<h2>How many add-ons failed only because they need an API key?</h2>
<p class="answer">On [[SMX:CD_FMT]], [[SMX:NEEDS_KEY]] installs out of [[SMX:TOTAL]] were separated into the "needs your key" bucket so credential-gated tools do not inflate broken counts.</p>
[[SMX:STATUS_BREAKDOWN_TABLE]]
<p>Our Nov 20, 2026 run (<code>src/_data/reports.json</code>) showed the same pattern: 25 installs across the five agents (5 per agent) failed strictly for missing keys, while 140 were broken and 4 were pending a second check. A "needs your key" result usually works fine once you supply your own token in the agent's config.</p>

<h2>How did each agent perform mid-week?</h2>
<p class="answer">Comparing each agent against a week earlier separates new breakage from persistent credential gates.</p>
[[SMX:AGENT_COMPARISON_TABLE]]

<h2>How do you check if an MCP server is safe before installing?</h2>
<p class="answer">Before pasting any MCP command into your terminal or <code>mcp.json</code>, verify three things: whether it passes a clean install check, what environment variables it asks for, and what local filesystem or shell permissions it expects.</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Check the nightly install log first.</b> Each listing on SkillMaxx shows whether an MCP server failed for a missing key (safe to configure with your own scoped token) or crashed on startup two nights running.</li>
<li><b>Inspect required arguments and env vars.</b> Servers that read local files or query databases (such as <a href="/skills/mcp-git/">MCP git</a> or <a href="/skills/yawlabs-postgres-mcp/">Postgres MCP</a>) should only receive the minimum directory path or read-only connection string they need.</li>
<li><b>Watch out for stale or unmaintained npm wrappers.</b> Checking <a href="/best/security/">security add-ons</a> and <a href="/reports/latest/">today's status</a> takes ten seconds before you grant an agent tool access.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What broke or got fixed [[SMX:SINCE]]?</h2>
<p class="answer">Confirmed breaks require two failing nights in a row; single-night dips stay pending until the next run.</p>
